    "<div style=\"background-color:rgb(160, 130, 200); padding:10px;\">\n",
    "\n",
    "## Understanding the Goal\n",
    "\n",
    "Before diving into data preprocessing, it's crucial to clarify our objectives. Sometimes, the task is clear from the outset, and other times, we need to explore the data through analysis, visualization, and exploratory data analysis (EDA) to identify the problem we can solve.\n",
    "\n",
    "- For today, we will focus on **Data Cleaning** and **Data Understanding**, ensuring that the dataset is ready for modeling.\n",
    "- The main goal for this dataset is to predict the  the Humidity -- ` Humidity`.\n",
    "\n",
    "## Let’s get ourselves familiar with some words/terminology:\n",
    "\n",
    "- **Feature** — Inputs of your model (the explanatory variables).\n",
    "\n",
    "- **Target** *(a.k.a. label, dependent variable)* — The output your model is trying to predict."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "27a3f940-d36e-4bde-a93a-bb3a18543fcf",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## 1.1 Do before the split (we will see examples of each in excercises and next lectures\n",
    "\n",
    "- ✅ **Check exact duplicate rows** (all columns match). Remove accidental copies; retain legitimate repeated observations. For this example, treat exact copies as duplicate records.\n",
    "\n",
    "- ✅ **Fix schema issues**:\n",
    "  - Fix obvious typos in column names.\n",
    "  - Drop pure constant columns (columns with the same value in every row).\n",
    "\n",
    "- ✅ **Remove impossible values** due to data entry errors  \n",
    "  (e.g., negative humidity), **when the rule is domain-deterministic** (no statistics learned).\n",
    "- Drop obvious IDs or row keys if they’re **leak-prone**.\n"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "fc7d1db3-c3ae-47f4-9b7a-d2021f3630b4",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "### Removing Duplicates\n",
    "\n",
    "First establish what one row represents. Remove accidental copies before splitting; separate observations may legitimately have identical values.\n",
    "\n",
    "For this example, treat exact copies as duplicate records and keep one copy. Repeated records of the same underlying observation must not appear in both training and evaluation sets.\n",
    "\n",
    "**See FAQ for more**"
   ]
  },
