   "source": [
    "## 1.2. Identifying target and Splitting dataset\n",
    "\n",
    "- **Training set** — Used to fit (train) the model.\n",
    "\n",
    "- **Validation set** — Used to tune hyperparameters and choose the best model and preprocessing pipeline.\n",
    "\n",
    "- **Test set** — Used only at the very end to report an unbiased estimate of final performance.\n",
    "\n",
    "\n",
    "Splitting the dataset into training, validation, and test sets is a crucial step in the machine learning pipeline. WHY?\n",
    "\n",
    "**Generalization:**\n",
    "- Generalization refers to a model's ability to perform well on new, unseen data. By splitting the dataset, we can train the model on one subset (training set) and evaluate its performance on another (validation and test sets). This helps us ensure that the model is not just memorizing the training data but is learning patterns that generalize to new data.\n",
    "\n",
    "**Avoiding Overfitting:**\n",
    "- Overfitting occurs when a model learns the training data too well, including its noise and outliers, leading to poor performance on new data. By using separate validation and test sets, we can detect and mitigate overfitting.\n",
    "\n",
    "**Model Evaluation:**\n",
    "- The test set provides an unbiased evaluation of the final model. It helps us understand how the model will perform in real-world scenarios.\n",
    "\n",
    "**Problem**\n",
    "\n",
    "We usually don’t have unlimited data. If we split into **train + validation + test**, sometimes each split is too small → unstable estimates.\n",
    "\n",
    "✅ **The solution**:  \n",
    "Instead of having a separate validation set, we can use **Cross-Validation (CV)** on the training data.\n",
    "\n",
    "**See FAQ for more**"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 11,
   "id": "fafe877b-11dd-459d-81f4-88f12a3aa83a",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
