   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "\n",
    "In Practice, we often use a **random split** to create training and test sets:\n",
    "\n",
    "- `test_size=0.2` → 20% of the data goes into the test set.\n",
    "- `random_state=42` → Sets a seed for the random number generator.  \n",
    "  This ensures **reproducibility**: every time you run the code, the split will be the same.\n",
    "\n",
    "⚠️ **But why not always use random splitting?**\n",
    "\n",
    "Random splitting creates train/test by shuffling rows; useful by default, but not for time series, grouped/clustered data (users/patients), or severe class imbalance—there you should use temporal, group-aware, or stratified splits."
   ]
  },
