    "## 1.3. Handling Missing Data\n",
    "\n",
    "Missing data can affect model fitting and interpretation.\n",
    "\n",
    "### Common Representations of Missing Data:\n",
    "- **NaN** (Not a Number)\n",
    "- Documented sentinel values, such as -99, -999 or ?\n",
    "- **Infinity**, which must be investigated before fitting\n",
    "\n",
    "\n",
    "- We have to  Use the <a href=\"https://pandas.pydata.org/docs/reference/api/pandas.DataFrame.replace.html\">DataFrame.replace()</a> function to replace all of the missing values (`np.nan`) by `Others`, `-99` or etc.\n",
    "\n",
    "### Strategies to Handle Missing Data:\n",
    "- **Removing rows with missing values**: This is a straightforward approach but can lead to the loss of valuable data.\n",
    "- **Imputation**: Filling in missing values with a specific value (mean, median, mode, or using a more complex method).\n",
    "\n",
    "### 💡 Suggestions:\n",
    "- **Remove observations:** sometimes appropriate, but discards information and may change which population is represented.\n",
    "- **Impute features:** use a mean, median, most frequent value or predictive model. Learn the rule from training data only.\n",
    "- **Review features with substantial missingness:** 30% is an inspection threshold here, not a universal rule for dropping columns. Consider why values are missing and what information the feature provides.\n",
    "\n",
    "\n",
    "Predictive imputation can use relationships between features, but does not automatically remove bias from \n",
    "non-random missingness. Do not impute missing targets and then treat them as observed labels.\n",
    "\n",
    "### Techniques for Handling Missing Data:\n",
    "- **Numerical Data**: Imputation using the mean, median, or mode.\n",
    "- **Categorical Data**: Imputation using the most frequent value or a e to Handle Missing Data\n",
    "der category.\n",
    "category.\n"
   ]
