    "## 1.4. Outlier Detection and Handling\n",
    "\n",
    "Outliers can significantly impact the performance of your machine learning model, particularly in regression tasks. Detecting and appropriately handling these outliers is crucial. We usually **drop the outliers**.\n",
    "\n",
    "#### We'll discuss outlier detection techniques and implement them in detail during our EDA session on Day 2.\n"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "4524df4a-d0c7-4c6e-8ae9-fb31d9ec16a5",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## 1.5. Data Scaling and Normalization\n",
    "\n",
    "Scaling and normalizing your data is a critical step in preparing it for many machine learning algorithms.\n",
    "\n",
    "### Why Scale and Normalize?\n",
    "- **Distances and penalties:** feature units affect nearest neighbours and coefficient penalties.\n",
    "- **Optimisation:** comparable scales can help gradient descent converge faster.\n",
    "- **Interpretation:** comparable scales do not make all features equally informative or influential.\n",
    "\n",
    "### Common Techniques:\n",
    "\n",
    "1. **Standardization (Z-score Normalization):**\n",
    "   - Centers the data by subtracting the mean and scales it by dividing by the standard deviation.\n",
    "   - $$X' = \\frac{X - \\mu}{\\sigma}$$\n",
    "   - **When to Use:**\n",
    "     - Most effective when data is normally distributed.\n",
    "     - Doesn't ensure an exact common range.\n",
    "     - Sensitive to outliers, which can distort relative distances between feature values.\n",
    "   - **Best For:** Algorithms that assume data is normally distributed (e.g., Linear Regression, Logistic Regression).\n",
    "\n",
    "2. **Robust Scaling:**\n",
    "   - Scales the data using the median and the Interquartile Range (IQR). This method is robust to outliers.\n",
    "   - $$X' = \\frac{X - \\text{Median}(X)}{Q3 - Q1}$$\n",
    "   - **When to Use:**\n",
    "     - Similar to standardization but more robust to outliers.\n",
    "     - Useful when data contains significant outliers.\n",
    "   - **Best For:** Algorithms where outliers can disproportionately affect the results.\n",
    "\n",
    "3. **Min-Max Scaling (Normalization):**\n",
    "   - Scales the data to a fixed range, typically [0, 1].\n",
    "   - $$X' = \\frac{X - X_{\\text{min}}}{X_{\\text{max}} - X_{\\text{min}}}$$\n",
    "   - **When to Use:**\n",
    "     - Ensures a fixed range, which is important for distance-based algorithms like K-Nearest Neighbors.\n",
    "     - Efficient regardless of the data's distribution.\n",
    "     - Doesn't reduce the effect of outliers.\n",
    "     - Doesn't correct the skewness of a distribution.\n",
    "   - **Best For:** Distance-based algorithms (e.g., K-Nearest Neighbors, SVMs) where the scale of data directly impacts the model.\n"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "9b613161-bc48-4cf9-93b5-38574479be0f",
