   "source": [
    "### Typical ML approaches type\n",
    "\n",
    "<img src=\"../imgs/ML_approaches.jpg\" alt=\"Description\" style=\"width: 1400px;\"/>"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "f6d44af8-a765-4180-8f15-1c0719b61103",
   "metadata": {
    "jp-MarkdownHeadingCollapsed": true
   },
   "source": [
    "###  Overview of Regression Models\n",
    "Regression models predict continuous target variables. Some common regression models include:\n",
    "- **Linear Regression:** Assumes a linear relationship between inputs and the target.\n",
    "- **Polynomial Regression:** Models nonlinear relationships by adding polynomial terms.\n",
    "- **Ridge Regression:** Adds an L2 penalty to prevent overfitting.\n",
    "- **Lasso Regression:** Adds an L1 penalty to shrink some coefficients to zero.\n",
    "- **Elastic Net Regression:** Combines L1 and L2 penalties.\n",
    "- **Support Vector Regression (SVR):** Fits the best line within a margin.\n",
    "- **Decision Tree Regression:** Splits data into regions using decision trees.\n",
    "- **Random Forest Regression:** An ensemble of decision trees.\n"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "30387d87-6981-4f35-8dee-873f0304fbdd",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "<div style=\"background-color:rgba(60, 179, 113, 0.3); padding:10px;\">\n",
    "    \n",
    "## Linear Regression in Machine Learning\n",
    "\n",
    "## Hypothesis Function\n",
    "The hypothesis function for linear regression is:\n",
    "$$ \\hat{y} = \\theta_0 + \\theta_1X_1 + \\theta_2X_2 + \\dots + \\theta_nX_n $$\n",
    "\n",
    "## Cost Function (MSE)\n",
    "To measure the accuracy of our hypothesis, we use the Mean Squared Error (MSE) cost function:\n",
    "$$ J(\\theta) = \\frac{1}{2m} \\sum_{i=1}^{m} (\\hat{y}_i - y_i)^2 $$\n",
    "\n",
    "## Minimizing the Cost Function\n",
    "The process of finding the best coefficients ($\\theta$) involves minimizing the cost function $J(\\theta)$. This optimization depends on the solver chosen.\n",
    "\n",
    "## Optimization methods:\n",
    "\n",
    "### Gradient Descent\n",
    "We minimize the cost function using gradient descent:\n",
    "$$ \\theta_j = \\theta_j - \\alpha \\frac{\\partial J(\\theta)}{\\partial \\theta_j} $$\n",
    "\n",
    "<img src=\"../imgs/GD.gif\" width=\"600\">\n",
    "\n",
    "\n",
    "### Normal Equation\n",
    "Alternatively, we can use the normal equation to find the optimal parameters:\n",
    "$$ \\theta = (X^TX)^{-1}X^T y $$\n",
    "\n",
    "### Singular Value Decomposition (SVD)\n",
    "When $X^TX$ is not invertible, we can use SVD to solve the normal equation.\n",
    "\n",
    "### Stochastic Gradient Descent Variants, Newton’s Method, Quasi-Newton Methods, Baysian, etc. \n",
    "### The optimisation method\n",
    "\n",
    "The objective tells us what to minimise. The **solver** tells us how to find the coefficients.\n",
    "\n",
    "- **Direct least squares:** solve the linear-algebra problem using a numerical method such as QR or SVD.\n",
    "- **Full-batch gradient descent (GD):** use all observations to calculate the gradient, then take a step downhill.\n",
    "- **Stochastic gradient descent (SGD):** update after each observation, usually shuffling the order between passes. Updates are cheaper, but noisier. A mini-batch averages a small group instead.\n",
    "\n",
    "<img src=\"../imgs/SGD.gif\" width=\"600\">\n",
    "\n",
    "- **Newton:** use the gradient and curvature of the objective to choose a step.\n",
    "- **Quasi-Newton:** approximate curvature from successive updates. L-BFGS is one example.\n",
    "\n",
    "</div>\n"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "e53247d2-6dfa-4801-83b3-16b36166f08c",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
