    "## Evaluatinga Metrics\n",
    "\n",
    "\n",
    "\n",
    "| Metric       | Equation                                                                 | When to Use                                                                 |\n",
    "|--------------|--------------------------------------------------------------------------|-----------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n",
    "| **MAE**      | $$ \\text{MAE} = \\frac{1}{m} \\sum_{i=1}^{m} \\lvert\\hat{y}_i - y_i\\rvert $$          | Use when all errors, large or small, have equal importance.                 |\n",
    "| **MSE**      | $$ \\text{MSE} = \\frac{1}{m} \\sum_{i=1}^{m} (\\hat{y}_i - y_i)^2 $$        | Use when you need to penalize large errors / sign of error not important.   |\n",
    "| **RMSE**     | $$ \\text{RMSE} = \\sqrt{\\frac{1}{m} \\sum_{i=1}^{m} (\\hat{y}_i - y_i)^2} $$| Use when you want to penalize large errors, but see it in the unit of the target. |\n",
    "| **R²**       | $$ R^2 = 1 - \\frac{\\sum_{i=1}^{m} (\\hat{y}_i - y_i)^2}{\\sum_{i=1}^{m} (y_i - \\bar{y})^2} $$ | Use when you want a general/comparable performance metric / units not required. |\n",
    "| **Max Error**| $$ \\text{Max Error} = \\max(\\lvert\\hat{y}_i - y_i\\rvert) $$                        | Use when you want to limit the magnitude of the errors.                     |\n",
    "\n",
    "### **Explanation:**\n",
    "\n",
    "- **MAE:** Provides a straightforward interpretation of the average error magnitude.\n",
    "- **MSE:** Useful for situations where larger errors are particularly costly, as it squares the errors before averaging.\n",
    "- **RMSE:** Gives a more interpretable error metric in the same units as the target variable.\n",
    "- **R²:** Offers insight into how well the model explains the variance in the target variable.\n",
    "- **Max Error:** Useful when it’s critical to minimize the worst-case eor adjust.\n"
   ]
  },
