    "## 3.2 Model Selection and Evaluation: Cross-Validation (K-Fold)"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "a9549f06-8eda-4f0c-9d12-92e578389344",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "- K-fold cross validation is typically used on the **training set** to guide our choices of data preparation and model parameters (using the test set would violate the philosophy of testing on unseen data).\n",
    "\n",
    "- Each fold learns imputation values, scales and coefficients using only its training portion, then predicts its validation portion without refitting.\n",
    "- Passing arrays already imputed or scaled using all training rows would let the validation folds influence preprocessing.\n",
    "- Cross-validation fits temporary models. Refit the chosen pipeline on the entire training set, then evaluate once on the test set.\n",
    "\n",
    "The code above followed this order. Below, inspect the fold scores computed before the final fit and test evaluation.\n",
    "\n",
    "See the [scikit-learn guidance on preprocessing and data leakage](https://scikit-learn.org/stable/common_pitfalls.html#data-leakage).\n",
    "\n",
    "- To make predictions, you typically follow these steps:\n",
    "\n",
    "   - Train the model on the entire training set: After cross-validation, you retrain the model on the full training data.\n",
    "   - Make predictions on the test set or new data: Once trained on the full training set, the model is ready to make predictions."
   ]
  },
