   "source": [
    "<div style=\"background-color:rgba(75, 0, 130, 0.3); padding:10px;\">\n",
    "\n",
    "**1. What does duplication mean, and by dropping them, are we not manipulating our dataset?**\n",
    "\n",
    "An exact duplicate has the same values in every column. Establish whether it is an accidental copy or a separate observation that happens to have identical values. Remove accidental copies; retain legitimate repetitions when they represent the population you intend to model.\n",
    "\n",
    "**2. Why do we handle duplicates before splitting the data?**\n",
    "\n",
    "Copies of the same underlying observation in training and test sets prevent an independent evaluation. Remove accidental copies before splitting, or keep related records together in a group-aware split. Identical feature values alone do not prove that two observations are copies.\n",
    "\n",
    "**3. How does splitting work for unsupervised models?**\n",
    "\n",
    "In unsupervised learning, we often don't have labeled data to split into training and test sets. However, we can still split the data to evaluate the model's performance. For example, in clustering, we can split the data and apply the clustering algorithm to the training set. Then, we can evaluate the clusters on the test set to see if the patterns found in the training set generalize to new data. We will be learning how to evaluate unsupervised models in the absence of labels in the coming days.\n",
    "\n",
    "**4. Using Machine Learning for Imputing Missing Data**\n",
    "\n",
    "Predictive imputation can use relationships between observed features and may improve on a mean or median. It still relies on assumptions about the available information and why values are missing. It does not automatically correct bias from non-random missingness. Fit the imputation model within each training fold and compare its effect using validation data.\n",
    "\n",
    "**5. How the SVD works?**\n",
    "\n",
    "See `computational-mathematics/lectures/lecture03/linear-algebra-2.ipynb`\n",
    "\n",
    "**6. What does negative $R^2$ mean?**\n",
    "\n",
    " When the $R^2$  is negative, it means that the model is performing worse than a simple baseline model that always predicts the mean of the target variable. Essentially, the model's predictions are so poor that they increase the error compared to this naive prediction.\n",
    "\n",
    "**7. Is there a trade off between MSE/RMSE and $R^2$?**\n",
    "\n",
    "On the same nonconstant evaluation targets, with the same observations and weights, lower MSE (or RMSE) always means higher $R^2$. For unweighted observations:\n",
    "\n",
    "$$R^2=1-\\frac{m\\,\\mathrm{MSE}}{\\sum_{i=1}^{m}(y_i-\\bar y)^2}.$$\n",
    "\n",
    "The denominator is fixed when comparing predictions on that set. Overfitting can improve training metrics while worsening test metrics, but does not create a trade-off between MSE and $R^2$ on the same set. Comparisons across different datasets or separately averaged fold scores need not follow the same ranking.\n",
    "\n",
    "**8. In Sckit learn if we want to use different optimization methods, we can use `SGDRegressor(loss, penalty, max_uter, learning_rate)`**\n",
    "\n",
    "**9. Show an example on how Gradient decent and normal equation works in linear regression without using Scikit learn.**\n",
    "\n",
    "</div>"
   ]
